        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the sesame oil in a Dutch oven or heavy pot over medium heat. Add the onion and cook for 3 minutes until it starts to soften."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the beef slices and stir briefly just until they change colour. Do not cook them through at this stage."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the potato, carrot, dashi, soy sauce, mirin, sake and sugar. Stir to combine. Bring to a boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to a gentle simmer. Skim any foam from the surface. Cover and cook for 20 to 25 minutes until the potato is tender when pierced with a skewer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste the broth and adjust seasoning. Ladle into bowls and garnish with spring onion. Serve with steamed rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Potatoes have dissolved into the broth",
              "cause": "Potatoes were cut too small or cooked too long at too high a heat",
              "fix": "Cut into large chunks and simmer gently. Check after 20 minutes. You want them soft but still holding their shape."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Broth is very sweet",
              "cause": "Too much mirin or the sugar was doubled by mistake",
              "fix": "Balance with a little more soy sauce and a splash of dashi to dilute the sweetness."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Beef is tough and chewy",
              "cause": "Beef was too thick or simmered for too long",
              "fix": "Thin-sliced beef needs less than 5 minutes in the broth. It should be barely pink when the vegetables are done."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Nikujaga is the dish most Japanese people associate with home cooking and their mother's kitchen. It appears in school cookbooks across Japan as a beginner recipe. The sweet-savoury broth is unlike any Western stew. It is eaten from deep bowls alongside rice. On cold nights, it is the definition of comfort."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
